From ff516964238ab2c3d7aef49205194f9a028eb3db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hank Yeomans <hank@quorra.ah.local>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 18:29:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 40/52] engram file layout: ahead-of-use row fetch for the
 second Engram layer (recipe T3.20 / E36, SGLANG_DSV41_ENGRAM_FILE_PREFETCH)

The two Engram layers' hash ids are a pure function of the input ids and
are both known before layer 0, so the first layer's eager break now does
one D2H of both layers' ids and hands the later layer's ids to its
_FileTable, whose single worker thread issues MADV_WILLNEED per distinct
page through libc (GIL released) and index_selects the rows into pinned
memory while layers 2-13 run. The later break only joins (ids compared
with torch.equal; a mismatch falls back to a synchronous fetch and is
counted in stats). H2D copies now come from pinned staging, non_blocking.

Modes (SGLANG_DSV41_ENGRAM_FILE_PREFETCH): ahead (default), sync (serial
WILLNEED per distinct page in the break), pool (the E18 per-row thread
pool, kept as the A/B comparator; SGLANG_DSV41_ENGRAM_FILE_THREADS now
applies to it only). The pool was the cost, not the help: CPython's
mmap.madvise holds the GIL, so 16 threads serialise on it and pay
0.65 ms warm for 48 rows against 0.02 ms on one thread (recipe E36).

Measured on the launcher default (219/165, chunk 8192, 64K x 4), one
serve per mode, KT timeline on: decode Engram break bs1 2.20 -> 1.80 ->
1.66 ms (pool / sync / ahead; the layer-14 break 0.94 -> 0.45), bs4 3.19
-> 2.15; C1 40.72/38.87 -> 41.47/39.55 wall; prefill 16K cold 1,592 ->
1,844 tok/s, 32K 1,596 -> 1,915 at the same ~1.5 % Engram residency;
NLL 0 of 65,653 moved, needles 6/6 at 55K, same-prompt C4 PASS.
Standalone GPU test: all three modes bit-identical to a plain
index_select reference at T = 1 / 4 / 300, hit and miss paths.

Amp-Thread-ID: https://ampcode.com/threads/T-01a0c10b-a5f8-77fc-b238-7147d12ccf29
Co-authored-by: Amp <amp@ampcode.com>
